I can't confirm 60 days. PropInspect's Texas guide says **30 days**.

- **Deadline:** The landlord has 30 days after the tenant moves out to refund the deposit or send an itemized list of deductions. The tenant needs to have given a forwarding address.
- **If the landlord is late:** From day 31, the law assumes the landlord acted in bad faith, and the landlord has to prove otherwise. Bad-faith withholding can cost $100, plus three times the amount withheld, plus attorney's fees.

The guide is based on Texas Property Code §§ 92.101–92.111 and was last reviewed on August 28, 2026, so I don't think it's outdated. I can't check the 60-day figure myself because I can only answer from this guide. It's worth asking your lawyer which law or lease term the 60 days comes from, since they may know something specific to your situation.

I know you said you don't need sources, but here's the link in case you want to show your lawyer: https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/texas. It also links to the official statute text.

This is general information, not legal advice. For your specific case, your lawyer's advice is what counts.